llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232): a
matrix-vector product reuses the q8_1 copy of its input that the previous
product of the same input made.

WHY
---
Every quantized matrix-vector product (mul_mat_vec_q, the path of
single-token decode) first converts its fp32 input to q8_1 in a kernel of
its own, also when the product right before it read the same input. Most
layers start with several such products: attention's q, k and v, and in a
Gated DeltaNet layer (Qwen3.5/3.6, Qwen3-Next) the qkv, z, beta and alpha
projections all read the same normalised hidden state. On an RX 590 a
dispatch costs at least 4.3 us however small the kernel (an empty kernel,
back to back on one stream), and these inputs are a few KiB, so each
repeated quantize costs about as much as a launch.

Measured on an RX 590 (llama-bench tg64, two alternating rounds, same
binaries otherwise):
  Qwen3 8B Q4_K_M, all on the GPU: 30.11 / 30.12 -> 30.33 / 30.75 tok/s.
  Qwen3.6 35B-A3B Q4_K_M, experts in system RAM (-ncmoe 99):
    24.20 / 24.03 -> 24.60 / 24.52 tok/s (run-to-run spread about 1 tok/s).
  llama-perplexity with -ub 1 (every token through mul_mat_vec_q) gives the
  same perplexity to the last printed digit on both models (8B 14.3290,
  35B-A3B 8.5860): the products read the same q8_1 bytes as before.
  test-backend-ops 16476/16476 against the CPU backend.

The repeated quantize is not specific to gfx803; only gfx803 was measured.

WHAT
----
ggml_backend_cuda_context keeps the q8_1 buffer of the last input that
ggml_cuda_mul_mat_vec_q quantized, with that input's data pointer, shape
and strides. A product whose src1 matches all of them on the main stream
skips the quantize kernel and reads the kept buffer. The buffer is dropped
at the start of every graph compute, before any node whose output overlaps
the kept input's bytes (each node of a fused group is checked), and
whenever a node runs on another stream. The q8_1 conversion does not
depend on src0's type, so products of different weight types share it.

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh
index 343f471..362c781 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh
@@ -1656,6 +1656,35 @@ struct ggml_backend_cuda_context {
     ggml_cuda_pool & pool() {
         return pool(device);
     }
+
+    // q8_1 copy of the last src1 that a matrix-vector product quantized, so the
+    // next product of the same input (q/k/v, a DeltaNet layer's input
+    // projections) skips its own quantize launch. Valid on the main stream
+    // within one graph compute, until a node writes over the input; declared
+    // last so it returns its memory before the pools are destroyed.
+    struct {
+        const void * data = nullptr;
+        size_t nbytes = 0;
+        int64_t ne[GGML_MAX_DIMS] = {};
+        size_t nb[GGML_MAX_DIMS] = {};
+        std::unique_ptr<ggml_cuda_pool_alloc<char>> q8_1;
+    } mmvq_src1;
+
+    void mmvq_src1_invalidate() {
+        mmvq_src1.data = nullptr;
+        mmvq_src1.q8_1.reset();
+    }
+
+    void mmvq_src1_invalidate_if_written(const ggml_tensor * t) {
+        if (mmvq_src1.data == nullptr) {
+            return;
+        }
+        const char * w0 = (const char *) t->data;
+        const char * c0 = (const char *) mmvq_src1.data;
+        if (curr_stream_no != 0 || (w0 < c0 + mmvq_src1.nbytes && c0 < w0 + ggml_nbytes(t))) {
+            mmvq_src1_invalidate();
+        }
+    }
 };
 
